## Migration: Thread.Abort → Cooperative Cancellation (.NET 8)

### Why This Breaks

In .NET 8, `Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` — it simply doesn't work. `ThreadAbortException` is never raised, `Thread.ResetAbort()` is obsolete (`SYSLIB0006`). You must switch to **cooperative cancellation**.

---

### Pattern Classification

| Usage | Pattern | Replacement |
|-------|---------|-------------|
| `_workerThread.Abort()` | Cancellable work loop | `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | Cleanup on cancellation | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Continue after abort | Check `token.IsCancellationRequested` |
| `GetNextItem()` blocking | Blocking call interruption | Pass token to unblock |

---

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Thread? _workerThread;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
        _workerThread.IsBackground = true;
        _workerThread.Start();
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = GetNextItem(token); // pass token to unblock
                ProcessItem(item);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Clean up partial work (same logic as the old ThreadAbortException block)
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                // No ResetAbort needed — just continue to let the loop exit naturally
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        if (_cts is null) return;

        _cts.Cancel(); // signal cooperative cancellation

        // Wait up to 2 seconds for graceful shutdown
        _workerThread?.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2));

        // If still alive after 2s, the thread is truly stuck.
        // There is NO equivalent to Abort(). Log and move on.
        if (_workerThread?.IsAlive == true)
        {
            // Log warning: thread did not exit within timeout
        }
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }
}
```

---

### Key Changes to Supporting Methods

Your `GetNextItem()` **must** accept a `CancellationToken` — otherwise cancellation can't interrupt it when it's blocking:

```csharp
// Before: blocks indefinitely, only Thread.Abort could interrupt
private WorkItem GetNextItem() => _queue.Take();

// After: throws OperationCanceledException when token is cancelled
private WorkItem GetNextItem(CancellationToken token) => _queue.Take(token);
// BlockingCollection<T>.Take(CancellationToken) supports this natively
```

---

### Critical Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Mitigation |
|---------|------------|
| **`GetNextItem` ignores the token** | Cancellation stalls. Every blocking call in the chain must forward the token. |
| **Not disposing `CancellationTokenSource`** | Causes timer/callback leaks. Implement `IDisposable`. |
| **Expecting instant stop** | Cooperative cancellation only fires at checkpoints. If `ProcessItem` takes 30s, it won't stop mid-work unless it also checks the token. |
| **Catching `OperationCanceledException` too broadly** | Use `when (token.IsCancellationRequested)` filter to avoid swallowing unrelated exceptions. |
| **No fallback for truly stuck threads** | There is **no** .NET 8 equivalent to force-kill a thread. If uncooperative code is unavoidable, move it to a child **process** and use `Process.Kill()`. |

---

### If You Can't Modify `GetNextItem`

If the blocking call is in code you don't control, use a timeout-based polling pattern:

```csharp
while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
{
    // Poll with short timeout instead of indefinite block
    if (_queue.TryTake(out var item, TimeSpan.FromMilliseconds(500)))
    {
        ProcessItem(item);
    }
}
```

This adds slight latency but guarantees the loop checks cancellation every 500ms.
